WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.660
How close can two points get before light blurs them into one?

2
00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:10.100
Inside cells, chromosomes part, filaments grow, and tiny motors step along tracks.

3
00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:15.740
Light microscopy lets us watch that motion, yet light itself sets a limit.

4
00:00:18.883 --> 00:00:21.943
A lens never images a point as a sharp dot.

5
00:00:23.383 --> 00:00:26.503
Light spreads into a bright centre with rings, an Airy pattern.

6
00:00:27.883 --> 00:00:32.303
Under the Rayleigh criterion, two points resolve when one centre falls on the

7
00:00:32.303 --> 00:00:33.523
other's first dark ring.

8
00:00:34.763 --> 00:00:37.003
That smallest separation is resolution.

9
00:00:38.217 --> 00:00:43.537
Resolution depends on emission wavelength λ, the wavelength a fluorescent label

10
00:00:43.537 --> 00:00:46.657
gives off, and numerical aperture, or NA.

11
00:00:48.017 --> 00:00:51.157
NA describes the cone of light the objective collects.

12
00:00:52.457 --> 00:00:56.697
In an ideal setup, a green label emits at 520 nm.

13
00:00:58.217 --> 00:01:03.457
An oil objective, NA 1.40, resolves 227 nm.

14
00:01:04.850 --> 00:01:07.390
Keep the same emission and swap objectives.

15
00:01:08.950 --> 00:01:12.650
Drop NA to 1.00, losing 0.4.

16
00:01:13.910 --> 00:01:16.210
The objective collects a narrower light cone.

17
00:01:17.450 --> 00:01:22.110
Will the smallest resolvable separation shrink, stay the same, or grow wider?

18
00:01:27.733 --> 00:01:34.753
It grows wider. A narrower cone widens each Airy pattern, pushing resolution to

19
00:01:34.753 --> 00:01:45.813
317.2 nm. Subtract 227 from 317.2: about 90 nm lost in this model.

20
00:01:46.867 --> 00:01:48.567
These are ideal values.

21
00:01:49.767 --> 00:01:54.587
The instrument's real response to a point, its point-spread function, can be worse

22
00:01:54.587 --> 00:01:56.747
with aberrations and thick samples.

23
00:01:58.167 --> 00:02:02.987
Suspected missing motor arms in cilia, for example, are confirmed by electron

24
00:02:02.987 --> 00:02:03.987
microscopy.

25
00:02:04.817 --> 00:02:07.017
Seeing motion also takes controls.

26
00:02:08.417 --> 00:02:13.097
In a gliding assay, motors stick by their tails to a coverslip under fluorescent

27
00:02:13.097 --> 00:02:18.817
filaments. Without ATP, nothing glides; add ATP, and filaments move.

28
00:02:20.177 --> 00:02:25.297
And purified systems lack the accessory proteins that change rates inside cells.

29
00:02:26.367 --> 00:02:29.527
Light microscopy then unlocked motion directly.

30
00:02:30.827 --> 00:02:36.847
In 1984, Mitchison and Kirschner inferred dynamic instability, ends switching

31
00:02:36.847 --> 00:02:40.687
between growing and shrinking, from microtubule populations.

32
00:02:42.287 --> 00:02:47.487
Microscopy of single ends soon showed switching directly at both ends of purified

33
00:02:47.487 --> 00:02:48.487
microtubules.

34
00:02:49.417 --> 00:02:54.497
Summers and Gibbons studied sea-urchin sperm axonemes, bundles of paired

35
00:02:54.497 --> 00:02:56.297
microtubules called doublets.

36
00:02:57.557 --> 00:03:01.097
Did the motor dynein slide doublets, or shorten microtubules?

37
00:03:02.297 --> 00:03:07.417
They briefly treated axonemes with trypsin, a protein-cutting enzyme, breaking

38
00:03:07.417 --> 00:03:09.137
structures that resist sliding.

39
00:03:10.437 --> 00:03:13.517
Adding 0 .1 millimolar, a T .P.

40
00:03:13.597 --> 00:03:16.857
scent doublets sliding out under dark-field microscopy.

41
00:03:16.857 --> 00:03:19.337
The answer: sliding.

42
00:03:20.550 --> 00:03:23.510
Tracking one dye even revealed motor steps.

43
00:03:24.930 --> 00:03:30.630
Kinesin-1 walks hand over hand along microtubule binding sites 8 nm apart.

44
00:03:32.010 --> 00:03:34.310
Its cargo moves 8 nm per step.

45
00:03:35.510 --> 00:03:40.770
The rear head swings past the front, so it moves two spacings: 2 × 8 = 16.

46
00:03:42.010 --> 00:03:47.750
In a 2004 imaging study, a dye on one head moved about 17 nanometers.
